# **A captured byte is not text, and git's line-ending normalisation does not know that.**
#
# `core.autocrlf = input` on the machine this was found on rewrote CRLF to LF inside
# `conform/fixtures/ghostty-1.3.1-scene01-attrs.vt` on the way into the index: 1949 bytes of what a
# terminal actually sent became a 1926-byte blob, and **every test still passed**, because the dump
# parser skips CR the way a terminal does. A fixture that is evidence, silently not being the
# evidence, is the exact failure `conform/` exists to prevent one level up.
#
# `-text` and not `binary`: `binary` is `-text -diff` together, and the diff is worth keeping — a
# capture that changes should be reviewable as a diff, which is the whole mechanism by which a
# report that reports rather than gates still catches a regression.
*.vt -text

# `fuzz/corpus/` is the same claim for the same reason: the committed corpus is the gate, and a
# corpus entry whose bytes were adjusted on the way into the index is testing something else.
fuzz/corpus/** -text

# A cursor report is not text either, and for the reason above. `.cpr` is the scene-05 channel:
# the terminal's own answers, read by `cursor_reports` rather than by the dump parser.
*.cpr -text

# And a DECRQM reply is not text, for the third time and the same reason. `.decrqm` is the scene-06
# channel: the terminal's own answers about mode 2026, read by `mode_reports`.
#
# **This line was missing from 2026-08-29, when scene 06 landed, until 2026-08-30.** No damage was
# done — none of the four captures committed in between contains a CR — and that is luck rather than
# safety, which is the same sentence the rule above was written to stop anyone having to say. The
# gap was found by the session that added a fifth `.decrqm`, which is one arm's worth of notice.
*.decrqm -text
